-------------------------
- Instantiating Objects -
-------------------------

All object instantiations look like this in Java:

new <ClassName>(<argumentList>)

There are three parts to an instantiation:

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	1. new				- the keyword new
	2. <ClassName>			- the name of the class being instantiated
	3. (<argumentList>)		- the argument list
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	Example :  new Scanner(System.in)

***

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Driver {
	public static void main(String[] args){
		Scanner input;
		input = new Scanner(System.in);
		
		System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
		String name = input.nextLine();
		
		System.out.println("Hello, " + name + "!");
	}
}

***

An instantiation is an expression that evaluates to the object that is instantiated (created). 
So, new Scanner(System.in) instantiates and evaluates to a new Scanner object. On line 06 this expression is the right 
hand side of an assignment statement. The value of this expression (the new Scanner object) is then assigned to the 
variable on the left hand side named input. The result of this line input = new Scanner(System.in); is that we now have 
a Scanner object named input that we can use to read and interpret input from the console keyboard.

Note that a Scanner object can be used to read and interpret input from many different input sources including the 
keyboard, disk files, and network connections. We generally pass the input source as an argument when we instantiate the 
Scanner object. In the code sample above we pass the System.in object as the argument, and the System.in object is 
associated with the console keyboard. So new Scanner(System.in) creates a Scanner object that reads from the keyboard 
as its source.

On line 08 we are using the System.out object to display some text on the console window. The System.out object knows 
how to do this, in fact the System.out object knows several ways to display text on the console window. In this case we 
are calling the System.out method named print.

On line 09, String name = input.nextLine(); we are using the Scanner object we created to read the user's input from the 
keyboard. We named our Scanner object input, and we are calling its nextLine method to read the next line of input from 
the keyboard. The nextLine method returns this input as a String, and we are storing this return value in the name 
variable.

On line 11 we are using another method of the System.out object, the println method. This method is exactly like the 
print method, except that it appends a newline character ('\n').

In this one 13 line Java program, we are instantiating and using one new object (a Scanner named input), and using two 
built-in objects (System.in, and System.out). This is object based programming - relying on some number of objects 
(each of which meets some responsibilities like displaying text on the console window or reading input from the 
keyboard). In object based programming, we write the code to make these objects work together (collaborate) to meet all 
the needs of our program.

***

The new keyword creates an instance object of a class.  This means it has it's own attributes and operations, which are 
not shared with other objects of the same class.

